      "paperId": "b06fce408d044064b84f0cd50c7282781e3ff759",
      "title": "Problems of philosophy of history in works of A.D. Sukhov and his philosophical searches",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Nikolay Chizhkov",
          "authorId": "1573604642"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "This article is dedicated to the memory of Andrei Dmitrievich Sukhov (1930\u20132025), a prominent figure in Russian philosophical scholarship who passed away at the age of 96. He made a significant contribution to the development of the Russian philosophical tradition. During his long and fruitful work at the Institute of Philosophy RAS, he left behind a significant legacy that deserves to be studied and analyzed. The article analyzes Andrei Dmitrievich's historiosophical concept, which combines a non-reductionist materialism with the dialectic of necessity and chance. The author demonstrates that the thinker understands the historical process as a multi-level movement in which economic, institutional, ideological, and other factors complement and influence each other, while thinkers, individuals, and intellectual groups are capable of acting autonomously and influencing the overall course of events. It is shown that Andrei Dmitrievich was focused on the idea of progress, which he defined as the qualitative expansion of the scope of human activity and forms of interaction. He viewed progress as a complex, and sometimes contradictory, development, with periodic leaps, stagnation, and setbacks.\nAn important part of his philosophical legacy is the thesis that philosophy has always been influenced by religious, political, and scientific factors. All of this determining the direction of philosophical creativity. He explored the principle of \"openness\" in Russian philosophy, which asserts that philosophical ideas arose at the intersection of theology, natural science, history, and literature. A characteristic example cited by Andrei Dmitrievich is analyzed: the \"materialistic philosophizing\" of Russian scholars (from Lomonosov to Pavlov and Tsiolkovsky). Along with an important area of his research concerning \"philosophizing historians\" (from Tatishchev to Klyuchevsky), demonstrating that historiosophical ideas organically grow out of the practice of historical science and imbue Russian philosophy with concreteness and a national-social dimension. He discovered a distinction between the descriptive and theoretical history of philosophy, where the former systematizes the material, and the latter was created to identify the internal laws of historical development and correlate them with the ideological structures, in our case, of Russian civilization.\n\u00a0",
